From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 05:44:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] domicile: logind owns the console, and Ctrl+Alt+F<n> asks it
 to switch

On a real panel, on a real tty, `Ctrl+Alt+F<n>` did nothing at all. The log
said `VT switching is on`, and it was: patch `0017` had the half that ANSWERS
a switch and nothing anywhere had the half that STARTS one. Both halves are
this patch, and the reason the missing one went unnoticed is that on every
machine without logind the kernel starts the switch for you.

`TakeControl` IS WHAT TURNS THE KERNEL'S OWN CHORD OFF, and patch `0020` calls
it because no ACL covers a keyboard. logind's `session_prepare_vt` then runs
`KDSKBMODE K_OFF`, `KDSETMODE KD_GRAPHICS` and `VT_SETMODE VT_PROCESS` on the
session's VT -- the three error strings are adjacent in any shipped
`systemd-logind` and were read out of one rather than assumed. `K_OFF` is the
console's chord handling, off. From the moment the desktop takes its input,
the only process that can start a console switch is the desktop. That is why
every Wayland compositor binds the chord itself, and it is what nothing here
did.

AND THE ANSWERING HALF WAS FIGHTING logind FOR THE VT. `0017`'s own
`VT_SETMODE` did not conflict with logind's -- the kernel overwrites `vt_mode`
and `vt_pid` with no `EBUSY`, so it SILENTLY TOOK IT. logind then never got
its release signal, never paused the devices it had lent this session, and
never handed the console over. A handshake installed on a VT logind already
owns is not a second opinion; it is a theft, and the only correct number of VT
ioctls in this fork is zero. `scripts/test-logind-owns-the-console.sh` counts
them, in the patches by net and in `src/` outright.

WHERE THE CHORD IS BOUND, WHICH IS THE DECISION THE REST FOLLOWS FROM. The
compositor advertises a `wl_seat` and reads no evdev node at all -- it pulls
neither libinput nor a session backend, by a decision `domicile-compositor`'s
`Cargo.toml` writes down -- so its seat is a promise to clients, not a source
of keys. Every key in the desktop arrives on the browser's evdev thread and is
dispatched by `EventFactoryEvdev`, which is a `PlatformEventSource`. So the
browser is the only process holding a keyboard descriptor and
`WillProcessEvent` is the first thing a key reaches: ahead of every dispatcher
and of any nested run loop's override, which is what makes a console switch
impossible to starve. It does not consume the press, and does not need to --
logind revokes the devices a moment later, so the shell sees a key that never
comes back up either way.

THE DISPLAY FOLLOWS THE SESSION, NOT A SIGNAL. `Active` on the session object,
read with a `Get` on every `PropertiesChanged` rather than parsed out of one:
logind names a changed property in the dictionary or in the invalidated list
depending on the property, and the table below ignores an answer that changes
nothing, so asking is both shorter and total. `Seat.SwitchTo(u)` on
`/org/freedesktop/login1/seat/self` is the other direction -- the seat and not
the session, because a chord names a console and `Session.Activate` cannot
express "whoever is on VT 3". logind's shipped polkit policy gives
`org.freedesktop.login1.chvt` `allow_active yes`, so none of it authenticates.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT REACH, said plainly because the ordering is the one thing a
reader will ask about. The card is NOT one of logind's devices -- the browser
opens `/dev/dri/card*` itself, through the ACL `70-uaccess.rules` does put on
it -- so no `PauseDevice` arrives for it and there is nothing to hold open
with `PauseDeviceComplete` the way libseat holds a DRM device. `Active` is a
statement about a switch logind has already made, so the drop is late by one
D-Bus round trip. Late is not wedged: the kernel restores its own framebuffer
when the last master goes, so the console is stale for that width rather than
black, and logind hands the VT over whatever this end does. Taking the card
from logind too is what closes it, and `A-DESKTOP-ON-A-TTY.md` carries it.

THE TABLE SURVIVED AND ITS IOCTLS DID NOT. `StepVtSwitch` is still a free
function for the reason `ModesetParamsFromSnapshots` is one, and it still
answers an asynchronous delegate against an edge that may have moved. Three
things changed. `kRefuseSwitch` is gone: there is nothing to refuse, so a drop
that fails is a log line and a display to take again rather than a console the
kernel is told to keep. `kConsoleWithoutDisplay` became
`kForegroundWithoutDisplay` and kept its job, which is that the way out stays
open. And the two RACES are new, because `Active` can flip twice before the
delegate answers once -- a console switched away from and straight back to
does exactly that. A take that lands after the session left gives the display
straight back, which is the two-owner bug arriving by the last door it had; a
drop that lands after the session returned asks for it again.

Fifteen cases, five of them over the chord. `Ctrl+Alt+Shift+F1` is somebody
else's shortcut and matching on the whole modifier mask rather than on two
bits is what keeps it theirs.

`0019` is untouched and is what makes any of this work: the drop still goes
through `DrmMaster`, in the process that opened the card, because the kernel
forbids it anywhere else. The comment there that promised a `VT_RELDISP(0)`
is the only line of it that had to change.

Guidelines audited: docs/guidelines/TESTING.md, docs/guidelines/ERRORS.md,
docs/guidelines/CONTROL_FLOW.md, docs/guidelines/FUNCTIONS.md,
docs/guidelines/FILES.md. Change complies. The D-Bus wiring itself has no
unit test, for the reason `DrmLogindInput` has none: it is glue over
`dbus::ObjectProxy` with no logic of its own, and every decision it makes is
in `StepVtSwitch` or `VtForChord`, which are tested. Nothing here compiles in
this container.

